\description{
National estimates of the percentage of the total population and selected
population groups experiencing deprivation in access to nutritious, quality
food in Mexico. The data cover five biennial observations from 2016 to 2024.
}
\details{
The data cover Mexico at the national level, with one reference year per
row, ordered from 2024 to 2016. Observations are biennial rather than annual.

All variables except \code{anio} are percentages on a 0–100 scale.
The denominator for \code{total} is the total population; each other
percentage uses the population of the named group as its denominator.
Group percentages do not represent shares of all people experiencing
food-access deprivation. Higher values indicate greater deprivation.

For example, \code{rural = 21.5541377089} in 2024 means that approximately
21.55 percent of the rural population experienced this deprivation.

The indicator is "Carencia por acceso a la alimentación nutritiva y de
calidad". It measures food-access deprivation under the source methodology
and should not be labeled simply as hunger.

The Indigenous population follows INPI criteria. It includes all members
of households in which the household head, spouse, or an ascendant relative
reported speaking an Indigenous language, together with Indigenous-language
speakers outside these households. Ascendant relatives include parents,
stepparents, grandparents, great-grandparents, great-great-grandparents,
and parents-in-law. This definition should not be interpreted as
Indigenous self-identification or ancestry alone.

Disability follows the source workbook's definition of inability or great
difficulty in at least one listed activity.

Missing values indicate unavailable estimates, not zero. Afrodescendant
population estimates are available only for 2024. Disability estimates are
available for 2020, 2022, and 2024.

Population groups overlap across classifications. For example, a child
aged 0–5 years can also be included in the rural population and the
Indigenous population. Percentages should not be summed across columns
or averaged without appropriate population weights to obtain an overall
deprivation rate. Differences between percentages are expressed in
percentage points.

The dataset contains point estimates only, without confidence intervals
or precision classifications.
}
